.Dd October 7, 2026
.Dt CAN 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm CAN
.Nd CAN Protocol
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In netcan/can.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket AF_CAN SOCK_RAW CAN_RAW
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the network layer protocol used on top of CAN bus networks.
At this time only the
.Dv SOCK_RAW
socket type is supported.
This protocol layer is intended to be compatible with the Linux SocketCAN
implementation.
.Ss ADDRESSING
A classical CAN frame consists of an 11-bit (standard frame format) or 29-bit
(extended frame format) identifier, followed by up to 8 data bytes.
CAN FD uses the same identifier formats with up to 64 data bytes.
The interpretation of the identifier is application-dependent, the CAN
standard itself doesn't define an addressing.
.Pp
The
.Nm
layer uses a 32bits identifier.
The 3 upper bits are used as control flags.
The extended frame format is selected by setting the
.Dv CAN_EFF_FLAG
control bit.
.Pp
The socket address is defined as
.Bd -literal
struct sockaddr_can {
        u_int8_t        can_len;
        sa_family_t     can_family;
        int             can_ifindex;
        union {
                /* transport protocol class address information */
                struct { canid_t rx_id, tx_id; } tp;
                /* reserved for future CAN protocols address information */
        } can_addr;
};
.Ed
For CAN raw sockets, the 32bits identifier is part of the message data.
The can_addr field of the sockaddr structure is not used.
.Ss MESSAGE
Classical CAN messages are defined as follows:
.Bd -literal
struct can_frame {
        canid_t can_id; /* ID + EFF/RTR/ERR flags */
        uint8_t can_dlc; /* frame payload length in byte (0 .. CAN_MAX_DLEN) */
        uint8_t __pad;
        uint8_t __res0;
        uint8_t __res1;
        uint8_t data[CAN_MAX_DLEN] __aligned(8);
};
.Ed
This structure remains 16 bytes, with the payload at offset 8.
For data frames, senders may also supply the 8-byte header followed by at least
.Va can_dlc
payload bytes, up to the structure size.
An incomplete header or data payload, or a length greater than 8, is rejected.
For a classical remote transmission request, the DLC specifies the requested
length and no payload is required after the header.
Applications should normally send the full structure.
.Pp
The lower 11 bits (for standard frames) or 29 bits (for extended frames) are
used as the on-wire identifier.
The
.Dv CAN_EFF_FLAG
bit is set in can_id for extended frames.
The
.Dv CAN_RTR_FLAG
bit is set in can_id for remote transmission request frames.
.Ss CAN FD
CAN FD messages use a separate structure:
.Bd -literal
struct canfd_frame {
        canid_t can_id;
        uint8_t len;
        uint8_t flags;
        uint8_t __res0;
        uint8_t __res1;
        uint8_t data[CANFD_MAX_DLEN] __aligned(8);
};
.Ed
This structure is 72 bytes, with the payload at offset 8.
CAN FD sends must supply the complete structure.
The
.Va len
field is a payload byte count from 0 through
.Dv CANFD_MAX_DLEN
(64), not an encoded wire DLC.
The software interface accepts every length in this range.
Mapping these lengths to a physical controller's DLC is the responsibility
of a future CAN FD hardware driver.
.Dv CANFD_MAX_DLC
is 15.
.Pp
The allowed
.Va flags
bits are
.Dv CANFD_BRS
(1, bit-rate switch),
.Dv CANFD_ESI
(2, error-state indicator), and
.Dv CANFD_FDF
(4, CAN FD frame marker).
Unknown flag bits and CAN FD frames carrying
.Dv CAN_RTR_FLAG
or
.Dv CAN_ERR_FLAG
are rejected.
The software loopback interface carries these flags as metadata;
it does not model electrical signaling or physical controller error state.
Received CAN FD records have
.Dv CANFD_FDF
set and their reserved bytes cleared.
.Pp
The integer socket option
.Dv CAN_RAW_FD_FRAMES
(option number 6) at level
.Dv SOL_CAN_RAW
is disabled by default.
Set it to a nonzero value to enable CAN FD sends and receives:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int enable = 1;

setsockopt(s, SOL_CAN_RAW, CAN_RAW_FD_FRAMES,
    &enable, sizeof(enable));
.Ed
An opted-in socket can still send and receive classical CAN frames.
Sockets without this option never receive CAN FD records.
Use a receive buffer large enough for a
.Vt struct canfd_frame
and inspect the returned record length to distinguish the two formats.
Applications must check system-call return values.
.Pp
The destination interface must advertise and enable
.Dv CAN_LINKMODE_FD
(0x10), with the CAN FD MTU of 72 bytes.
Use
.Xr canconfig 8
to inspect capabilities and change the mode while the interface is down.
An opted-in socket alone does not enable CAN FD on an interface.
Classical-only interfaces cannot transmit a CAN FD record.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr canloop 4 ,
.Xr netintro 4 ,
.Xr canconfig 8 ,
.Pa /usr/include/netcan/can.h
.Pp
.Lk https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SocketCAN "SocketCAN - Wikipedia"
.Lk https://www.kernel.org/doc/Documentation/networking/can.txt "Readme file for the Controller Area Network Protocol Family"
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
protocol appeared in
.Nx 8.0 .
.Sh BUGS
The CAN FD extension provides a software socket and
.Xr canloop 4
contract.
It does not add a physical CAN FD driver, data-phase timing configuration,
ISO-TP transport, or error-frame support.
Virtual checks do not establish physical bus interoperability or real-time
behavior.
